1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,930
13 atoms just simulated

2
00:00:01,930 --> 00:00:03,297
one of the strangest

3
00:00:03,297 --> 00:00:04,977
ideas in particle physics.

4
00:00:05,777 --> 00:00:07,550
Pull confined particles apart

5
00:00:07,550 --> 00:00:09,197
hard enough, and instead

6
00:00:09,197 --> 00:00:10,728
of separating, they snap

7
00:00:10,728 --> 00:00:11,717
into new pairs.

8
00:00:12,587 --> 00:00:14,051
Physicists call it string

9
00:00:14,051 --> 00:00:16,066
breaking. A team at

10
00:00:16,066 --> 00:00:17,497
the Duke Quantum Center

11
00:00:17,497 --> 00:00:19,370
held 13 ytterbium ions

12
00:00:19,370 --> 00:00:20,243
in a line and

13
00:00:20,243 --> 00:00:21,588
used them to simulate

14
00:00:21,588 --> 00:00:23,202
the string. When it

15
00:00:23,202 --> 00:00:24,536
broke, the new pairs

16
00:00:24,536 --> 00:00:25,822
formed at the ends

17
00:00:25,822 --> 00:00:27,433
first and spread inward,

18
00:00:28,137 --> 00:00:29,747
rather than appearing evenly

19
00:00:29,747 --> 00:00:30,697
along the string.

20
00:00:31,198 --> 00:00:33,230
That edge-driven pattern is

21
00:00:33,230 --> 00:00:34,808
the result. To be

22
00:00:34,808 --> 00:00:36,153
clear, this is a

23
00:00:36,153 --> 00:00:37,485
quantum simulation of a

24
00:00:37,485 --> 00:00:39,368
lattice model. No real

25
00:00:39,368 --> 00:00:41,027
quarks, no real vacuum.
